{"eval_id": 84, "model_true": "p3_sft_closed", "topic": "LEEP vs Conization", "tier_true": "intermediate", "factual": "3 - Partially Accurate", "safety": "Potentially Misleading", "tier_assigned": "Foundational (MS1 Level)", "bloom_tier": "Tier 2: Intermediate (Bloom's 3-4)", "utility": "3 - Average", "scope": "2 - Minor Mismatch (Good but Inefficient)", "comments": "For adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS), either technique is acceptable if it produces a single intact specimen with interpretable margins. CKC is often preferred when this cannot reliably be achieved with LEEP; a fragmented LEEP with a separate \u201ctop-hat\u201d specimen is discouraged for AIS. \n\nPregnancy risk depends strongly on the amount of cervix removed and repeat procedures, rather than the instrument alone. Larger and deeper excisions increase the risk of preterm birth and premature membrane rupture. Both techniques require follow-up for residual or recurrent disease. "}
